course: everyday
lesson: 78
title: Eyes, nose and using the toilet
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Eyes are precious and cannot be replaced: lasers and chemicals can hurt them for life, so we never point lasers at anyone and we wear safety glasses for experiments.
explain: The nose and the bottom are places where many germs live, so we use tissues and toilet paper, throw them away and wash our hands afterwards.
explain: Good toilet habits keep us and the next person clean and well: wipe from front to back, flush only paper, close the lid, wash hands and tidy up.
explain: Some body troubles are common and nothing to be ashamed of, like constipation or wetting the bed, but some signs, like blood in wee or poo, always need a doctor.
explain: A girl in Wellington whose eyes are red, sticky and itchy tells her mum and uses only her own towel, so her conjunctivitis does not spread to her sisters.
fact: Lasers can damage sight, so a laser pointer is never pointed at anyone's eyes.
fact: Red, sticky, itchy eyes may be conjunctivitis, which spreads easily, so you tell a parent and do not share towels.
fact: Carrots are healthy for the eyes but do not give night vision; that story came from wartime propaganda, a made-up story spread on purpose during a war.
fact: Safety glasses are worn for experiments and chemicals, because eyes can be hurt for life.
fact: Picking your nose spreads germs and can cause nosebleeds, so you use a tissue and wash your hands.
fact: A runny nose is blown gently into a tissue, one side at a time, and the tissue goes in the bin.
fact: Wiping from front to back after the toilet stops germs from the bottom reaching where you wee.
fact: After the toilet you flush, close the lid if there is one and wash your hands.
fact: Only toilet paper is flushed, and only as much as needed, because wipes, toys and too much paper block the pipes.
fact: Younger children go to public toilets with an adult or a friend, never with a stranger.
fact: Fruit, vegetables, whole grains and water help with constipation, which is common.
fact: Accidents and bed-wetting happen to many children; it is not their fault and parents can help.
fact: Blood in wee or poo needs checking by a doctor, so you tell a parent.
exercise: Your friend wants to shine a laser pointer at a classmate's face. What should you say? | Never point it at eyes, lasers can damage sight | It is fine if it is only for a second or two, as a joke | Lasers only hurt animals' eyes | It is safe if the light is red
exercise: Your eyes are red, sticky and itchy. What should you do? | Tell a parent and do not share towels | Rub them hard with your fingers to clean them | Share your towel so others can check | Put on your friend's sunglasses
exercise: Do carrots let you see in the dark? | No, they are healthy but give no night vision | Yes, eating a few carrots every day gives you night vision | Yes, but only orange carrots | No, carrots are bad for the eyes
exercise: Your class is mixing chemicals in an experiment. What should you wear when told to? | Safety glasses | Dark sunglasses from home | Nothing, glasses get in the way | A cap pulled down low
exercise: Why should you not pick your nose? | It spreads germs and can cause nosebleeds | It makes your nose grow bigger and bigger over the years | It stops you smelling for a week | It makes your nose run more
exercise: How should you blow a runny nose? | Gently into a tissue, one side at a time, then bin it | As hard as you can with both sides at once | Into your sleeve, and keep going until it stops running | Sniff it all back up instead
exercise: How should you wipe after using the toilet? | From front to back | From the back towards the front | Only from side to side | It does not matter at all
exercise: What should you do when you have finished on the toilet? | Flush, close the lid and wash your hands | Flush and go straight back to class | Leave without flushing to save water | Wash your hands only if they look dirty afterwards
exercise: You missed the toilet and made a mess. What should you do? | Clean it up or tell an adult | Leave it for the next person to find | Leave quickly before anyone sees | Blame it on someone else who was there
exercise: What may be flushed down the toilet? | Only toilet paper, and only what you need | Wet wipes, which melt away in the water just like paper | Small toys that are broken | Anything that fits down the hole
exercise: You are seven and need the toilet at the shopping centre. Who goes with you? | An adult you know or a friend, never a stranger | Any kind stranger who offers to take you there | Nobody, you go alone | The shop's security guard only
exercise: You have not been able to poo for days and it hurts. What helps? | Fruit, vegetables, whole grains, water and telling a parent | Eating only white bread and crisps | Drinking nothing at all until it passes on its own | Keeping it secret and waiting
exercise: You wet the bed. What should you do? | Tell a parent calmly, it happens to many children | Hide the wet sheets somewhere so that nobody ever knows | Never drink anything again | Feel ashamed and say nothing
exercise: You see blood in your poo. What should you do? | Tell a parent, it needs checking by a doctor | Flush quickly and forget it | Eat more sweets to fix it | Wait a whole month and see if it goes away
test: A boy in Ohio says carrots let pilots see in the dark. What is true? | That story was wartime propaganda, carrots are healthy but give no super sight | It is true, pilots ate carrots to see at night | It is true for all orange vegetables | It is true only for children
test: Why must girls especially wipe from front to back? | It keeps germs from the bottom away from where you wee | It uses less toilet paper than wiping from back to front | It is quicker | It keeps the toilet seat clean
test: A girl flushes a wet wipe because it says «moist». What could happen? | It could block the pipes | It would dissolve like toilet paper | It would scrub the pipes clean on the way | Nothing, wipes are made to flush
test: Which is true about bed-wetting? | It happens to many children and is not their fault | Only babies ever wet the bed | It means the child is lazy and does not bother to get up | It cannot be helped in any way
test: What would probably happen if a child with conjunctivitis shared a towel with her brother? | Her brother could catch it | Her eyes would get better faster | The towel would become clean | Nothing, it only spreads by air
test: Why does a doctor need to know about blood in wee? | It can be a sign of something that needs treatment | It means you drank too much water | It always comes from eating beetroot | Doctors collect such facts about children just for fun
test: A boy keeps picking his nose in class. What is the better habit? | Use a tissue and then wash his hands | Pick it only when the teacher looks away | Wipe his finger on the desk | Pick it with a pencil instead
test: Why is a safety glasses rule important in a chemistry experiment? | A splash could hurt the eyes for life | Glasses help you read the instructions | Chemicals smell less with glasses on | The glasses keep the hair tidy
test: A friend says the best thing for constipation is to stop eating. What is true? | Fruit, vegetables, whole grains and water help | Stopping eating for a day or two cures it at once | Eating only cheese helps most | Sweets make it pass faster
test: got a laser pen, gonna shine it in jacks eyes for a laugh | No, lasers can damage sight | Yes, if it is only for a second | Yes, small lasers are harmless | Yes, if Jack closes one eye first
test: grandad says eat carrots n you can see in the dark. true? | No, carrots are healthy but give no night vision | Yes, carrots let you see in the dark like an owl | Yes, if you eat them raw | Yes, but only orange carrots
test: dropped my dinosaur in the toilet, just flush it | No, only toilet paper is flushed, toys block the pipes | Yes, toilets can take small toys | Yes, flush twice to be sure it goes all the way down | Yes, if the toy is soft
test: my eyes r red n sticky n itchy | Tell a parent, it may spread, use your own towel | Rub them hard with your sleeve till they feel better | Share a towel to wipe them | Ignore it, it is nothing
explain: A boy in Texas rubbed his eye after cutting chillies and later after playing in sand; the eye got red, sore and sticky, and he needed drops from the doctor.
explain: A girl in Dublin always held in a wee for hours at school; she got a urine infection with pain and a fever and needed medicine.
fact: Rubbing an eye with dirty hands can scratch it or carry germs into it, causing a painful infection.
fact: If sand or dirt gets in your eye, you do not rub it; you blink, rinse it with clean water and tell an adult.
fact: Holding in wee for a long time often can lead to urine infections, so you go when you need to; urine is the grown-up word for wee.
test: sand in my eye, rubbing it hard | Stop rubbing, blink and rinse with clean water, tell an adult | Rub harder and faster with your knuckle until the sand comes out | Poke it out with a finger | Close it and sleep with it in
test: teacher said wait till break but i really gotta go | Ask again politely, holding it a long time can cause an infection | Hold it all day, it is good practice | Never ask, it is rude | Drink more water so it goes away
test: lil bro shoved a bead up his nose lol | Tell an adult now, don't poke it, it may need a doctor | Push it further up so it comes out of his mouth instead, easy | Get him to sniff hard | Leave it, it melts in a day
test: some kid shone a laser pen in his mate's eye and they say he's totally blind now, what's the real lesson | Never point a laser at anyone's eyes | Lasers are only ever dangerous at night | Close your eyes and lasers cannot hurt you | Only big lasers can hurt, pens are fine
test: my gran swears eating carrots lets u see in the dark like a cat, true? | No, carrots are healthy but do not give night vision | True, after ten carrots you see in the dark | True, but only raw carrots work | False, carrots are actually bad for the eyes
test: everyone's saying Tom wet the bed on the school trip, should i pass it on | No, it happens to many kids; keep it to yourself and be kind | Yes, everyone should know what happened, it's only fair to tell | Yes, but only tell people in your room | Yes, and give him a nickname for it
exam: Identify the principal reason for wiping from front to back subsequent to use of the toilet. | It prevents germs from the bottom reaching where one urinates | It uses less toilet paper than any other method | It is quicker and so saves time | It keeps the toilet seat cleaner for others
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the items that may properly be flushed down a toilet? | Toilet paper only, and only as much as is needed | Toilet paper together with wet wipes | Any item small enough to pass the bowl | Paper towels and tissues in any quantity
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning red, sticky and itchy eyes. | They may indicate conjunctivitis, which spreads readily | They are a sign of tiredness and require no action | They are cured by rubbing the eyes firmly | They indicate that more carrots should be eaten
exam: Determine the appropriate course of action whereas blood is observed in a child's urine or stools. | Inform a parent, as a doctor must examine it | Disregard it, as it always resolves unaided | Drink less water until it disappears | Conceal it, since it is a cause for shame
